Sure, here's an example of how to generate a table of contents (TOC) automatically in a LaTeX document:

1. Start your LaTeX document with the standard document class and preamble:

   ```
   \documentclass{article}
   \usepackage{tocloft} % optional package for customizing TOC
   \begin{document}
   ```

2. Use the `\tableofcontents` command after the `\begin{document}` command to generate the TOC:

   ```
   \tableofcontents
   ```

3. Add the `\section` commands to create the document's sections:

   ```
   \section{Introduction}
   ...
   \section{Methods}
   ...
   \section{Results}
   ...
   \section{Discussion}
   ...
   ```

4. To customize the TOC formatting, you can use the `tocloft` package (optional):

   ```
   \usepackage{tocloft}

   % Set TOC font size and spacing
   \renewcommand{\cftsecfont}{\normalfont}
   \renewcommand{\cftsecpagefont}{\normalfont}
   \setlength{\cftsecnumwidth}{2em}
   \setlength{\cftsecindent}{0pt}
   \setlength{\cftbeforesecskip}{1em}

   % Set TOC title font size and spacing
   \renewcommand{\contentsname}{\huge Table of Contents}
   \renewcommand{\cfttoctitlefont}{\Huge}
   \renewcommand{\cftaftertoctitle}{\vspace{1em}}
   ```

   Here, we've set the font size and spacing for section entries and the TOC title. You can customize these values to suit your document's style.

5. Compile your LaTeX document with the following command:

   ```
   pdflatex myfile.tex
   ```

   This will generate a PDF file with a TOC that includes all the sections you've added to your document.

Note: The specific commands used to customize the TOC formatting may vary depending on the document class and packages used in your LaTeX document. This example uses the `article` document class and the `tocloft` package for customization, but other packages like `titletoc` or `tocbasic` can also be used. Consult the LaTeX documentation for more details.

