When comparing strings in Java, you should always use the `.equals()` method instead of the `==` operator. This is because the `==` operator checks for reference equality, which means it compares the memory locations where the strings are stored. Two strings with the same content but stored in different memory locations will not be equal when compared with `==`.

The `.equals()` method, on the other hand, compares the actual values of the strings, regardless of their memory location. This allows you to compare strings for equality, even if they are stored in different memory locations.

Here's an example to illustrate the difference:

```java
String str1 = "hello";
String str2 = "hello";

// Using == operator
if (str1 == str2) {
    System.out.println("str1 and str2 are equal (using ==)"); // false
} else {
    System.out.println("str1 and str2 are not equal (using ==)");
}

// Using .equals() method
if (str1.equals(str2)) {
    System.out.println("str1 and str2 are equal (using .equals())"); // true
} else {
    System.out.println("str1 and str2 are not equal (using .equals())");
}
```

